WEBVTT

00:00:00.016 --> 00:00:02.942
We will start with the result shape of the inner query.

00:00:02.942 --> 00:00:11.627
Then we will learn scalar subqueries, followed by set-oriented patterns such as IN, EXISTS, ANY, and ALL.

00:00:11.627 --> 00:00:18.622
After that, we will study correlated subqueries, where the inner query depends on the current outer row.

00:00:18.622 --> 00:00:22.652
Finally, we will compare placements and rewrites.

00:00:22.652 --> 00:00:28.362
This order makes subqueries easier to understand because every new pattern answers a specific kind of

00:00:28.362 --> 00:00:34.092
question. Keep a real project in mind as we work through this section—for example,

00:00:34.092 --> 00:00:38.307
a hospital identifying patients above a benchmark or with no follow-up.

00:00:38.307 --> 00:00:45.242
The technical feature matters because it changes what questions we can answer reliably and how easily another

00:00:45.242 --> 00:00:48.762
analyst can understand our work.

00:00:48.762 --> 00:00:53.372
As you listen, try to connect each SQL or data concept to a decision you might need to

00:00:53.372 --> 00:00:59.392
defend later: what the data means, what one row represents, what can be missing,

00:00:59.392 --> 00:01:03.429
and what assumptions the transformation introduces.
